see you know how did you manage to say  say Stella differently I whispered it  well whispered it with with true pain  you know some some films it's really  nice to have barely controlled chaos  going on no real idea particularly where  the camera is going to be no idea when  it's gonna be on you just do it and and  and you do a see what happens and sort  of almost you know go start filming  before you're ready and and that works  very nicely for for that film and then  other films where it has it has to be  much more plot driven and so you do need  a bit more definition  you know everything needs everything  needs some sort of an obstacle and  hurdles to define it you know I think  that I've certainly never worked on  anything that has no hurdles and  obstacles at all because then I think  that you know you'd still be work I'd  still be working on it now no one would  ever stop at all with Ridley he's able  to make it in 74 days without  compromising quality I think because  first off he has a team who were really  fantastic who he works with again again  again again again now a lot of directors  do that but then Ridley is very  charmingly impatient right which again  is another nature of directors but he  will work with multiple cameras and  there'd be some scenes not just with  action shots but there might be scenes  in Joel and I would be sitting like this  and there would be six cameras and and  and and I've really enjoyed that because  you didn't ever have to stop to go back  and do oh we've got to get jobs close-up  now now I've got to get my close-up now  we've got to get the double shot you're  getting everything at the same time no  repetition is needed no matching of  takes is ever needed whatsoever you can  just do whatever you want each and every  time it can be very different and and  also he marries this real art  you know he's a painter that's what he  loves to do he sketches non-stop on coal  she gets a stick out puts it in the sand  and sketches to describe to us you know  what he wants  he marries that with it with a real  practical no-nonsense approach to  filmmaking and to having real momentum  you don't sit around much on on Ridley  sets whereas other directors you you can  find yourself sitting around for days  and that doesn't happen were really  Ridley and I were talking about making  the film whilst I was making American  hustle and I walked in the office I was  looking more like a bald Santa Claus  more than Moses and you know and he  looked very shocked and looked like what  the hell has he done here and said no  you got very short hair you know and  tried to avoid looking at my girth and  then you know he must have just been  racing through his brain of okay can I  get somebody else can John T Yates  create a tunic big enough to hide this  and I just tried to lose as much weight  as I could and the tunics were very  helpful and then I continued losing  weight as we as we filmed as well but it  also led us to say well look Moses is  such a fascinating character and and and  both red and I were surprised ourselves  in how complex he was and that he does  he really does go through almost every  human emotion possible and and going far  beyond you know our film you know  we have to find a place to finish at  some point but we we were interested in  keeping on going and making an 8 hour  long film just a I'm not sure anyone  else would have been interested in that  at all but we did we said well are we  really going to get hung up on hair you  know in such a incredible story and  obviously it spans a great deal of time  so hence you know short hair and then  he's got in dreadlocks and he hacks it  and you know sometimes the beard is long  sometimes it's sure and and and and that  and that to me is an example of you know  these hurdles which actually helped to  define a film and and and in the end  enhance it has it created either more of  a spiritual identity or more of a  political consciousness being part of  this I mean I think inevitably that  politics is within the world that we're  showing absolutely an important role and  mixed in with religion but also the the  thing that was very fascinating to me in  in the Torah was the nature of Moses  relationship with God and like he's  described in the film you know that the  definition of Israel Israel is he who  wrestles with God and and Moses did that  non-stop you know he didn't want the job  he initially said no again and again and  again God got quite mad at him there's  actually a passage where God tries to  kill Moses Mizzy pora  saves him by circumcising one of their  sons and and there's that there's that  turmoil throughout and ultimately ending  tragically with Moses not making it to  the promised land to to Canaan and and  so it is it is a story of revolution and  conflict my takeaway if you call it that  from this movie because I was right I  was raised Catholic and this story to me  when I was a child was more of a fable  you know all I saw when I read stories  of the Bible was the animals two by two  and the parting of the Reds  I was mystified by it by those stuff and  I had a young the beginnings of a young  scientific mind and I would always but  how was that possible and oh well if God  made that happen and then I started  having this relationship with God which  was very much guilt driven when my  parents who were only 35 at the time  were just young kids really trying to  raise two boys and and were obviously  tired or you know they've been up too  late on a Saturday so Sunday morning and  two young eager kind of you know  church-going boys who they had created  by sending us in our Holy Communion  let's go to church not this morning so  after a few missing weeks of going to  church I was terrified of going back to  this little wooden house where God was  and and so my relationship with religion  was that coming to this movie what I see  is the reason why those stories are  there what it's there to teach us and  yes you're saying it's the story of a  struggle and a struggle for freedom and  in the inverse of that really is that  you know my characters there to  represent the opposite ethical point of  view that we should have as either a  human or as the leader of other humans  is that one culture should respect  another culture that one person should  respect another person and that we time  and time again don't and that's why this  story is so resonant so the real take  away from me is not so much about  increasing my faith but increasing my  opinion of religion as something that  should be there for the powers of good  and unfortunately so many times that  those ideas and beliefs butting up  against each other cause too much damage
 

